{
 "thread": "GqPpQR5ZQeY",
 "subject": "Multiple outcomes nested across domains",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2018-03-19T10:01:20Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "XWAtHrKOBwAJ",
   "from": "James Rooney",
   "author": "f9c6a2087c744460",
   "date": "2018-03-18T10:46:03Z",
   "subject": "Multiple outcomes nested across domains",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">HI Paul,<br>\r\n<br>\r\nMany thanks for putting together such a great package.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI was wondering is it possible to build multivariate models with multiple outcomes nested in domains as described here: <a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3031784/\" rel=\"noreferrer nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3031784/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792451386689000&amp;usg=AOvVaw02J2zVI-aPYPb8NyRZg-wH\">https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3031784/</a><br>\r\n<br>Such models have been extended to longitudinal models (<a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4863889/\" rel=\"noreferrer nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4863889/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792451386689000&amp;usg=AOvVaw13yJhEyHvDGZnGBmuMCi1x\">https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4863889/</a> ), models with levels for treatment vs control etc. This paper has an example with Stan code -<br>\r\n<br>\r\nPaper: <a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/hbm.23052\" rel=\"noreferrer nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/hbm.23052&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792451386689000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0yKTTOGQGN8uWZCz8ubuLI\">https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/hbm.23052</a><br>\r\n<br>\r\nCode: <a href=\"https://zenodo.org/record/33432#.Wq403q2cbK\" rel=\"noreferrer nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://zenodo.org/record/33432%23.Wq403q2cbK&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792451386689000&amp;usg=AOvVaw13WVD5PzLiscMG7CHEwZQF\">https://zenodo.org/record/33432#.Wq403q2cbK</a>  - although that code does not use a true multivariate distribution.<br>\r\n<br>Many thanks,<div>James</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "vw5740vCAQAJ",
   "from": "Paul Buerkner",
   "author": "6e3dd74b422c061b",
   "date": "2018-03-18T11:31:06Z",
   "subject": "Re: [brms-users] Multiple outcomes nested across domains",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>As far as I understand the paper, the multiple domain model can not yet achieved with brms, because there is no syntax to define coefficients of different response variables<br></div>to be the same. That is, they will *not* share coefficients, which I understand is needed for the multiple domain model(?)<br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">2018-03-18 11:46 GMT+01:00 James Rooney <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jpkr...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span>:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">HI Paul,<br>\r\n<br>\r\nMany thanks for putting together such a great package.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI was wondering is it possible to build multivariate models with multiple outcomes nested in domains as described here: <a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3031784/\" rel=\"noreferrer nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3031784/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792451386690000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0Ddfay5RGrC55mGkl_JOIA\">https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3031784/</a><br>\r\n<br>Such models have been extended to longitudinal models (<a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4863889/\" rel=\"noreferrer nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4863889/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792451386690000&amp;usg=AOvVaw20mEm9z0_VRzGJunZMWm-i\">https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4863889/</a> ), models with levels for treatment vs control etc. This paper has an example with Stan code -<br>\r\n<br>\r\nPaper: <a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/hbm.23052\" rel=\"noreferrer nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/hbm.23052&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792451386690000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2A1yo4JukI4tis3tO03yUC\">https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/hbm.23052</a><br>\r\n<br>\r\nCode: <a href=\"https://zenodo.org/record/33432#.Wq403q2cbK\" rel=\"noreferrer nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://zenodo.org/record/33432%23.Wq403q2cbK&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792451386690000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1WclxvrylxkBmbOH65LfdT\">https://zenodo.org/record/33432#.Wq403q2cbK</a>  - although that code does not use a true multivariate distribution.<br>\r\n<br>Many thanks,<div>James</div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
