  {
   "id": "PXw9ZvoLAgAJ",
   "from": "Paul Buerkner",
   "author": "6e3dd74b422c061b",
   "date": "2018-03-19T10:01:20Z",
   "subject": "Re: [brms-users] Multiple outcomes nested across domains",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Currently the rescor parameters cannot be predicted and it will be hard to implement that. There is already an issue for it on github I think.<br><br></div>Your model might be possible in the future, but I need to come up with a good syntax to equate parameters across formulas.<br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\">2018-03-19 9:51 GMT+01:00 James Rooney <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jpkr...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span>:<br><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I previously answered this and my message was deleted. Perhaps because I posted an image with and equation ?<div><br></div><div>Anyway, My understanding, which is limited, is that the model is set-up like a mixed model pooling outcomes by domain (equations 1 &amp; 2 in Thurston paper). One thing that strikes me is that &#39;domain&#39; is a variable defined across outcome columns - i.e. you can&#39;t include a new column with domain in your dataset because it is not a row-wise property.</div><div><br></div><div>I was wondering Paul, is it possible to define an outcomes nested in domains hierarchical structure into the <i>rescor</i> parameters of the multivariate model ?</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks,</div><div>James</div><span><div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div></div></div></span></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><span><div><div><br><br>On Sunday, March 18, 2018 at 11:31:06 AM UTC, Paul Buerkner wrote:</div></div></span></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><span><div><div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>As far as I understand the paper, the multiple domain model can not yet achieved with brms, because there is no syntax to define coefficients of different response variables<br></div>to be the same. That is, they will *not* share coefficients, which I understand is needed for the multiple domain model(?)<br></div></blockquote></div></div></span></div></blockquote></div></div>"
